## 5. The cheapest baseline

The cheapest baseline is a test we get for cheap or near nothing on the same data. No training is needed and it should run in a few minutes (see Table 1). For example copy-last (predict the next frame as the current one), episode length (use the episode length to determine the outcome of an episode) or the commanded joints (select the action chunk whose commanded joints are closest to the joints measured at the goal). For these tests, we do a paired bootstrap: both the paper's method and the baseline are scored on the same data, we resample the episodes 2,000 times and compare the scores with a 95 % interval.
Out of all claims, {{b_n}} had a baseline test and {{b_lost}} lost to it, {{bs_loses}} of them with an interval below zero:

- LeWorldModel and DINO-WM vs actions alone. The goal is for the method to pick the chunk the robot really executed. The baseline just picks the chunk whose commanded joints are closest to the goal. LeWorldModel picks it at most {{b_lewm_top1}} of the time and DINO-WM slightly more, but the baseline picks it {{ex_c_base}} of the time (depending on how far ahead it is looking), a gap of {{b_lewm_gap}} points for LeWorldModel and {{b_dino_gap}} for DINO-WM, at every distance. The baseline is strong because our arm goes almost exactly where it is commanded, so the commanded joints already say where it will end up.
- ContactGuard vs the current latent. The goal of the model is to predict failure from the imagined future latent. The baseline just uses the current latent to predict failure: {{b_cg_b}} vs {{b_cg_m}} AUROC. The gap is {{b_cg_gap}}, and the interval {{b_cg_ci}} is below zero.
- Robometer vs episode length: the goal of the method is to judge the success or failure of the task. But it loses to the baseline, which only checks the duration of the episode ({{ex_b_len}} vs {{ex_b_ours}} on ArmnetBench). This is a problem we discovered on ArmnetBench, where the operators stop a success when it is done but let a failure run on.

The last one, adaptive chunking, was actually considered uncertain because the confidence interval {{b_aac_ci}} contains zero, meaning in some resamples it is better than the fixed chunk length and in others it is worse. Also, with only {{b_aac_n}} episodes, there is too little data to decide, so we do not count it as a real loss.

As for ContactGuard's paper results, we found the opposite. The goal of ContactGuard is to imagine the future latent with the world model and predict failure or success from this imagined latent. The compared baseline just encodes the current frames as a latent, skips the imagination and uses the same probe as with imagination. So the question becomes: is it really useful to imagine the future to determine failure or success? From the paper, it is a yes (AUROC 0.98 vs 0.84), but a no from our tests ({{b_cg_m}} vs {{b_cg_b}}).

One thing we discovered is that the choice of the baseline decides the verdict of the claims. For example, LeWorldModel predicts the next latent much better than the copy-last baseline ({{b_lewm_pred}} vs {{b_lewm_cl}}), so this claim holds. But on the test of picking the executed chunk, the model performs worse than the commanded-joints baseline. Basically, predicting well is not equal to planning well, and with just the first baseline we would have concluded that the claim was a pass.

But baselines are not equal; some are extremely strong because of a quirk. For example, on ArmnetBench, successes are stopped when they are done and failures run on, so the episode length baseline is very strong. But on our own episodes, which do not have this quirk, it is near chance ({{b_len_own}}). This also means that analysing the baseline results rigorously is very important, as a baseline could be far too strong.

In our case, baselines were particularly strong because our tasks are short and simple (for any task only 1 or 2 joints usually move). On long-horizon tasks, a learned latent model has more to give. Where the papers report their own cheap baseline, the methods still beat it on our data: PACT-VLA (against a random pick) and HWM (against net joint displacement).

In practice, a baseline needs to be crafted with care, but after that, if the model cannot beat it, then we can stop. If it beats it, then it also needs to beat the bar to be recognized as a validated claim.

Table 3: The paper claims we compared with a cheap baseline, on the same data.

| method | baseline (what is measured) | method | baseline | outcome | paired difference, positive = method better [95 % interval] |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| LeWorldModel | copy-last (latent error at 30 steps, lower is better) | 0.31 | 0.89 | beat | {{bs_ci_c0011}} |
| LeWorldModel | actions alone (executed chunk ranked first) | 0.60 | 0.68–0.90 | lost | {{bs_ci_c0012}} |
| DINO-WM | actions alone (executed chunk ranked first) | 0.61 | 0.68–0.90 | lost | {{bs_ci_c0021}} |
| RoboJEPA | copy-last (9-step latent error) | 11.8 % below copy-last | – | beat, under its bar of 15 % | {{bs_ci_c0031}} |
| ContactGuard | the current latent (failure AUROC) | 0.70 | 0.82 | lost | {{bs_ci_c0061}} |
| ReAP | shuffled actions (failure AUROC) | 0.789 | 0.708 | beat | {{bs_ci_c0081}} |
| Robometer | episode length (failure AUROC, ArmnetBench) | 0.818 | 0.938 | lost | {{bs_ci_c0102}} |
| HWM | net joint displacement (cosine to the macro-action) | 0.608 | 0.551 | beat | {{bs_ci_c0162}} |
| Adaptive chunking | fixed chunk length (boundary jump, lower is better) | 0.973 | 0.940 | lost | {{bs_ci_c0192}} |
| PACT-VLA | a random pick (error of the executed window) | −0.27° against random | – | beat | {{bs_ci_c0201}} |
| REMORA | per-frame readout (readout flips) | 0.70 × per-frame | – | beat, under its bar of 0.5 × | {{bs_ci_c0221}} |

## 6. Can the model run on the hardware fast enough?

Time to generate a chunk is critical for real-world robotics. The generation of a chunk should at least be done before the previous chunk is executed, and that is the bare minimum. The problem is that when the paper is tested in sim or on a big GPU (cloud or desktop GPU), the latency numbers do not hold on small hardware.
When testing papers we use our edge GPU (the Orin), which is also the main inference system, so a latency claim fails if the method cannot run on it fast enough.

So what fits on our Orin, and what made it better?

- SmolVLA: {{tm_smol_pt}} in PyTorch (too slow), but {{tm_smol_cpp}} with vla.cpp, the inference engine we used in our tests, which was {{tm_smol_x}} faster than PyTorch. So the choice of the inference engine was really useful.
- VLA-JEPA: {{tm_vjepa}} per chunk.
- Fewer denoising steps (horizon × depth): 2 steps instead of 10 saves only {{tm_hwm_save}} ({{tm_hwm2}} vs {{tm_hwm10}} ms), because the vision part alone takes {{tm_vis}}.
- Real-time chunking: the smoothing trick costs only {{tm_rtc}}.

What did not fit?

- RoboJEPA's encoder: {{tm_rj}} vs {{tm_rj_bar}} required.
- Methods that sample many chunks and pick one get expensive without batching, because our engine runs the samples one after another:
  - Adaptive chunking: {{ex_a_ours}} per decision vs a {{ex_a_bar}} budget, since it pays for 20 full passes.
  - PACT-VLA: about {{tm_pact}} per cycle (estimated) ({{tm_pact_t}} control ticks vs 8).

These two fail on our engine, not necessarily on the method: with batching, they might fit.

So the lesson is that latency is one of the simplest checks, and it tells you cheaply whether there is a need to invest in a claim. And of course it is possible to improve it, for example by choosing the right inference engine, using batching or lowering the denoising steps, but the hard limit that needs to be met is the time to execute one chunk. And it is testable without any training.

## 7. Why they failed here: the method, our data, or our setup

For each failure, Table 4 says whose method failed and what on our side may explain it. Most failures have at least one such cause, so a failure here is a failure on this rig, with this data, and not a verdict on the method in general.

Our data is small and simple. We have {{tel_train}} training episodes of one short task, about {{tel_ep_s}} s each, where the papers use hours of varied data; HWM, for instance, uses about 130 h against our 0.6 h. On a short task with one arm, the commanded joints already hold most of the state, which leaves little for a latent to add. The task is also easy for our policy, since SmolVLA is at its ceiling on it, so there is little uncertainty for an entropy rule to read: the chunk length chosen by adaptive chunking sat at its minimum in 86 % of decisions. For H-JEPA, our task lies outside the regime the paper targets, as there is no gap between slow and fast parts of the scene, so we count its failure as out of scope rather than a failure of the method.

We also have too few failures. Our policies mostly succeed, and the main rollout set has only {{ro_p_fail}} failures in {{ro_p_eps}} episodes. A bar such as five failures caught out of eight is coarse, and a single episode can change the verdict (ReAP, VLA-Corrector, SURVEYOR).

In {{t_p_var}} papers, and in the monitor claim of SURVEYOR, our version of the method is not exactly theirs. For FAIL-Detect and SAFE we used our world model's surprise as the score instead of their own scores; their code needs the internal features of the policy that ran each episode, and only {{fs_refwd}} successes and {{fs_refwd_f}} failures of ours could be re-run through it. Our first conformal band was also built wrong, calibrated on the same episodes it was fitted on; with the band built as the papers build it, {{fs_fp}} of successes are flagged at α = 5 % (not {{fs_fp_old}}) and {{fs_tp}} of failures are caught, and both claims still fail. For SURVEYOR [@surveyor2026], the drafter is not released, so its monitor claim ran with our own model's imagination, and REMORA's learned progress model was trained on 8 H200 GPUs, so we tested only its model-free readout, on three progress levels read from hand-written cues instead of its 201 learned bins. These are failures of our variants, and we do not claim that the authors' methods fail. Four more are counted as the authors' method, fitted to our rig: we kept their recipe and changed only what our rig or data forced. ContactGuard runs with two cameras instead of three and mixed policies, and the threshold claim of SURVEYOR uses its rule as written. VLA-Corrector keeps the paper's features, network, label window and gate, trained on 30 rollouts per fold instead of 120 episodes, with the failure onsets read from video and no stage head. ReAP's Stage 1 detector and Stage 2 predictor are rebuilt from the paper (no code is released), on a smaller DINOv2, one camera, and training data that is mostly other tasks (ArmnetBench); it caught {{reap_caught}} of 8 failures at {{reap_fi}} of 42 false interventions.

Our inference engine matters as well. vla.cpp samples sequentially and has no batching, so adaptive chunking pays for 20 full passes, while the paper's 106 ms was measured with batching on an A800. For PACT-VLA, the time of the check is projected rather than measured. Finally, several tests were offline only: planning ranked recorded chunks, and chunking measured smoothness on recorded episodes. None of these ran in closed loop on the arm.

Table 4: Each failed or not-proven paper, whose method failed, what we measured, and what on our side may explain it.

| failed paper | whose method | our result | what may be on our side |
|---|---|---|---|
| LeWorldModel | authors' | planning rank 0.60, action-only 0.68–0.90 | one short task, 157 episodes, one seed; offline ranking only |
| DINO-WM | authors' | planning rank 0.61, action-only 0.68–0.90 | same data as LeWorldModel; one training |
| RoboJEPA | authors' | 11.8 % below copy-last, bar 15 % | near miss; the 1B model reached 15.8 % on 30 clips only |
| H-JEPA | outside its regime | progress ρ −0.73, bar −0.80; false stalls 52 % | our task is outside the paper's regime |
| HWM | not proven | not proven: high level worse at 2 s | 0.6 h of one task against their 130 h |
| ContactGuard | authors' | AUROC 0.70, current latent 0.82 | mixed policies and 2 cameras against one policy and 3 cameras |
| FAIL-Detect and SAFE | our variant | {{fs_fp}} of successes flagged at α 5 % (papers' band) | our score, not theirs; 11 + 11 successes to build the band |
| ReAP | authors' method, reduced | {{reap_caught}} of 8 failures caught | Stage 1 and 2 rebuilt from the paper, smaller DINOv2, one camera, mostly other tasks; one seed of five; 8 failures |
| VLA-Corrector | authors' | {{vc_caught}} of 8 caught at {{vc_fi}} of 42, AUROC {{vc_auroc}} | 30 training rollouts per fold against their 120 episodes; our onset labels, no stage head; 8 failures |
| Robometer | authors' | AUROC 0.818, length 0.938 | zero-shot only; on ArmnetBench failures run longer than successes (on our rollouts length is near chance) |
| SURVEYOR | authors' (threshold); our variant (monitor) | threshold 1.249 against 0.20; 3 of 8 | their drafter not released; 8 failures |
| REMORA | our variant | readout flips 0.70 of the per-frame readout | hand-written progress atoms |
| Adaptive chunking | authors' (latency: our engine) | 4,751 ms per decision; length 2 in transport and at the grasp | sequential engine, no batching; task at ceiling for our policy |
| PACT-VLA | our engine | 37 ticks of delay against 8 | check time projected; sequential engine |
| Real-Time Chunking | not proven | not proven: 71–73 % smoother, no arm run | offline smoothness only |
| Horizon × depth | not proven | not proven: 0.78, bar 0.70 | one policy, one task; arm test not run |

## 8. Limitations

Our data covers one short task family, about {{tel_ep_s}} s per episode, with one arm, two cameras and about {{tel_min}} minutes of demonstrations, so it says nothing about long-horizon tasks or varied scenes. There are few failures to test the monitors on, {{ro_p_fail}} in the main set of {{ro_p_eps}}. Many methods are our own variants rather than the authors' code end to end (Table 4), and the planning and chunking tests ran offline, never in closed loop on the arm. A single operator ran the arm and labelled the outcomes without being blind to them, many tests use small samples and a single training seed, and few results come with a confidence interval; a bootstrap over episodes for the baseline comparisons covers all {{bs_have}} comparisons, but {{bs_near}} of these intervals come from a re-run that matches the stored result only to the second or third decimal. Two VLA results have no numeric bar and rest on 10 trials. The bars are ours, not the papers'. We chose the papers from the stack we run, so the rates describe these papers and not the field. Finally, our own mistakes are possible.

## 9. Related work

Two works evaluate policies on the same arm: a real-world VLA benchmark with a taxonomy of failures and recoveries [@2606.08881], and ArmnetBench, a fleet of SO-101 cells that evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. In simulation, vla-eval runs VLAs across 14 benchmarks [@2603.13966], and another benchmark studies the quality of demonstrations [@2610.18869]. These works rank policies. We instead take the claim a paper makes about its method and test it against a bar fixed in advance, on one cheap rig. The closest work is RoboSeed [@2610.40026], which scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, including reproduction evidence and deployment affordability; its authors note that the score is not a probability that a result is correct. We give one verdict per claim on one rig, with the data behind it and the reason we think it failed.

## 10. Conclusion

We re-tested {{c_paper}} claims from {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a rig of about €{{rig_eur_round}}, with under ${{cost_round}} of cloud GPU. {{c_paper_repro}} held, and {{t_c_auth}} failed with the authors' method on our data. The most useful part was the cheapest baseline: the same world model could pass one baseline and fail another, and {{b_lost}} of the {{b_n}} methods we compared with one lost to a signal we had for free, although only {{bs_loses}} of these losses have an interval below zero, of which LeWorldModel's, DINO-WM's and ContactGuard's are clean cases with the authors' method. Our advice to anyone re-testing a paper on a cheap rig is to run that baseline first, on their own data, with the bar fixed before the test. Everything is public, and every number can be rebuilt from the repository. Authors who think a failure here is ours (Table 4) are welcome to tell us, and we will correct it.

